KEY RESULTS (3-seed, publication-grade):
1. ours all-seen 0.914 / all-para 0.826, oldest-S0 forgetting +0.000 — matches R30 (0.918) exactly.
   The lifelong stream is retained in a SINGLE dense checkpoint, no gold, no inference memory.
2. Every in-weights NO-REPLAY baseline forgets catastrophically (age-graded): naive 0.408,
   continued-FT-with-GOLD 0.460, loramerge 0.367. => REPLAY, not the training signal, drives
   retention: continued-FT has a gold advantage over ours yet forgets far worse (0.460 vs 0.914).
3. LoRA-merge does NOT match ours (0.367 vs 0.914) AND destroys base capability (hop 0.205->0.062):
   sequential adapter-merge without replay both forgets and damages the base model.
4. ours (NO inference memory) BEATS external memory on BOTH seen (0.914 vs 0.875) and para
   (0.826 vs 0.690): knowledge-into-weights via replay matches/beats a retrieval bank while
   dropping the inference-time dependency. (extmem is also less stable: seed-2 cumulative scaffold
   transiently collapsed at r2-r3 to ~0.1-0.38 then recovered — the R17 memory-collapse failure
   mode; ours is monotone/stable across all rounds.)

Passes all pre-registered pass/fail (codex): ours all_seen>=0.88 & S0-forget<=0.05 & hop-drop<=0.03;
no-replay baselines forget >>0.15; LoRA-merge does not match ours; external memory recall is a
strong-teacher / inference-memory artifact, not knowledge-into-weights.

BOTTOM LINE: the contribution is now COMPARATIVE — replay-consolidation into a single dense
checkpoint beats standard continual-learning baselines (sequential no-replay, continued-FT-with-gold,
LoRA-merge) on lifelong retention with no inference memory and no joint retraining, and matches/beats
external memory without needing memory at inference. Does NOT prove growth necessity (that is the
grokking probe, next). Pod 80bxl5ul5ofyll terminated.

